From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Juan Pablo Lassala <jpablolassala@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 12:00:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] panel: Ask Caminito to hide the panel while a window overlaps
 it

A panel for a dock should get out of the way when a window covers it, and
come back when none does. mate-panel's own autohide hides whenever the
pointer leaves, and a panel can't tell when a window overlaps it, since a
Wayland client knows no window's geometry.

Add Intellihide to a panel's Properties, beside Autohide. With it on, the
panel reserves no space, and asks Caminito, the Wayland compositor, through
caminito_intellihide_manager_v1, a private protocol on its layer surface
(ADR 0047), to hide it. Caminito knows every window's geometry: it hides the
layer surface off its edge while a window on the panel's output's active
workspace overlaps it, and shows it when none does or when the pointer rests
at that edge. The panel is never told that it is hidden, and draws as usual.
The request is the protocol object's existence, so turning the option off,
unmapping the panel, or moving it to another monitor, which makes another
layer surface, ends it and asks again.

The protocol's two interfaces are written out in wayland-intellihide.c, from
caminito/resources/caminito-intellihide-v1.xml in Caminito's source, to keep
wayland-scanner out of the panel's build: the panel only makes requests, and
the protocol has no events. The key is a new one in the toplevel schema,
"intellihide", false by default.

The gate is the global: only Caminito offers it. Without it the checkbox is
not shown, the panel asks for nothing and reserves its edge as it did, and on
X11 nothing changes. Intellihide and Autohide turn each other off in the
dialog, and Floating, which is on top of this, goes below Intellihide.
